  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/l1zembgzqrwnnxakykyldrkhullifh-aynay-nxm6k-banh7-hp7x2",
      "html": "<ul><li>From the Betta rubra complex</li><li>IUCN Red List: Vulnerable</li><li>Wild caught</li><li>Maximum size: 2.25 inches (about 5.7 cm)</li><li>Temperament: somewhat territorial, but can be kept in groups as long as there is enough space</li><li>Breeding: mouthbrooder</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/l1zembgzqrwnnxakykyldrkhullifh-aynay-nxm6k-banh7-hp7x2",
      "html": "<p>Be sure to have the habitat ready before purchase.</p><p>Betta dennisyongi and Betta rubra are some of the easiest wild bettas to start out on, as males show a lower level of aggression and they can do well in community tanks.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/l1zembgzqrwnnxakykyldrkhullifh-aynay-nxm6k-banh7-hp7x2",
      "html": "<p>We recommend a pair per 5 gallons (about 19 litres) of water.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/l1zembgzqrwnnxakykyldrkhullifh-aynay-nxm6k-banh7-hp7x2",
      "html": "<ul><li>TDS: ~60–100 (can tolerate high TDS water but prefers low TDS water)</li><li>pH: ~6.0–7.0 (prefers softer water)</li><li>Temperature: 75–83°F (about 24–28°C); does well in cooler temperatures, but higher temperatures induce coloration and breeding behaviours</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/l1zembgzqrwnnxakykyldrkhullifh-aynay-nxm6k-banh7-hp7x2",
      "html": "<p>Carnivorous. Currently feeding on live foods but can be trained to eat frozen and dried foods. The seller currently feeds blackworms, frozen bloodworms and pellets.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/l1zembgzqrwnnxakykyldrkhullifh-aynay-nxm6k-banh7-hp7x2",
      "html": "<p>Somewhat territorial, but can be kept in groups as long as there is enough space. Males show a lower level of aggression than many bettas.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/l1zembgzqrwnnxakykyldrkhullifh-aynay-nxm6k-banh7-hp7x2",
      "html": "<p>Both males and females have a red-brown base body with black markings on the head and body. Fully grown adult males have longer anal and caudal fins than females. Both sexes become bright red with blue iridescence on the edge of the fins. Unlike many species in the genus, females can be as brightly coloured as the male. Males can be told apart by the more intense coloration on the fins and body, often with more iridescence, while females show egg-spots when ready to breed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/l1zembgzqrwnnxakykyldrkhullifh-aynay-nxm6k-banh7-hp7x2",
      "html": "<p>Mouthbrooder. Higher temperatures induce breeding behaviours.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/l1zembgzqrwnnxakykyldrkhullifh-aynay-nxm6k-banh7-hp7x2",
      "html": "<p>B. dennisyongi is differentiated from Betta rubra by different black facial markings and less intense black bars on the body. The two are very close genetically and are only truly differentiated by location.</p>"
    }
  ],
